# Line endings for files Linux EXECUTES.
#
# The blobs in this repository are LF. That is not enough. Most of this fleet
# works on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites these files to CRLF
# on checkout AND in `git archive` - so a Windows agent building the Ubuntu
# package ships CRLF into it without ever seeing a modified file in git status.
#
# A shebang line ending in \r makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally
# named "bash\r". Measured 2026-09-09 on this laptop:
#   packaging/build-deb.sh: line 3: set: pipefail: invalid option name
# and the same defect in packaging/DEBIAN/postinst is a package that installs
# and then fails to configure, on the GCloud instance, at the end of the runbook.
#
# eol=lf pins the working-tree form regardless of the checkout platform.
#
# This file is the UNION of two branches that each added rules for the same
# reason: PR52 for the Debian packaging, and the walkthrough work for the shell
# tools and their fixtures. Neither set is redundant, so both are kept.

*.sh                             text eol=lf
*.bash                           text eol=lf
*.py                             text eol=lf

# Debian maintainer scripts and control files: dpkg runs these as /bin/sh.
packaging/DEBIAN/preinst         text eol=lf
packaging/DEBIAN/postinst        text eol=lf
packaging/DEBIAN/prerm           text eol=lf
packaging/DEBIAN/postrm          text eol=lf
packaging/DEBIAN/control         text eol=lf
packaging/DEBIAN/conffiles       text eol=lf

# Installed onto the target as an executable and a unit file.
packaging/blackboard-bus         text eol=lf
packaging/blackboard-bus.service text eol=lf
packaging/env.example            text eol=lf

# Fixtures compared byte-for-byte by tests. A CRLF rewrite here does not break
# an interpreter, it breaks an assertion, and the failure names the wrong cause.
/tests/fixtures/order_supervisor_board.json  text eol=lf
/tools/walkthrough/fixtures/*.txt            text eol=lf

# CLIPS rule files, for the same reason as those fixtures rather than for tidiness.
# tools/board_governor/governor.py READS collision.clp and concatenates it into the
# generated program, and tests/mutate_board_governor.py deletes guards from it by
# EXACT substring match. CRLF would not break the rule engine - it would make every
# mutation anchor miss, so the harness reports ANCHOR-MISS instead of a verdict and
# a silently untested guard looks identical to a tested one. Every sibling .py here
# is already pinned; this file was the one unpinned text file in the tool.
*.clp                            text eol=lf
